  "description": "O3' selects a block, but NOT the bound state's block. The objective t_lambda = w_lambda * chi^lambda(W_atom) factors into an atom-INDEPENDENT weight and an atom-dependent character; measured over all six committed rows, argmin(t) == argmin(w) every time, so the character never moves the selection. Two atoms at n=3 and two at n=9 give the SAME block with DIFFERENT prices. Mechanism UNEXPLAINED: the obvious spread-dominance account is refuted here -- chi-spread exceeds w-spread at n=9 and the argmin is unmoved anyway. CONSEQUENCE: PR #6752's reading of the selection as 'which Wedderburn blocks the bound state saturates' is WRONG and is corrected; the pricing result (y_0* nonzero and atom-dependent, where the full LP has y_0* = 0 identically) STANDS.",
